                "reviews": [
                    {
                        "recommendationid": "238207979",
                        "author": {
                            "steamid": "76561198371128503",
                            "num_reviews": 8,
                            "playtime_forever": 3192,
                            "playtime_last_two_weeks": 1127,
                            "playtime_at_review": 2989,
                            "last_played": 1794125552
                        },
                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "(EDIT) The game is getting better and better.  It must be the best dinosaur hunter game.  They just added hit-box practice in the shooting range (awesome and a cool way to reanimate trophies), a JEEP (transforms it from a tedious walking simulator), and a scent mechanic.  I appreciate the feedback requests and frequent updates.  That is refreshing.\n\nWhen I first started playing this game, I would have given it a 5.5/10 (partly do to missing features, and partly due to ME not realizing I could do things, just needed to buy the right gadgets in the shop).   Now, knowing more and especially after they added some features, I feel like this is a 7.8/10 game. \n\nThe best part of this game, I think, is the T-rex *behavior*.  Every other dinosaur behaves somewhat more generic, but the T-rex was programmed to be a cunning, stalking predator that is aware if you have identified him or not.   He even plays dumb on me, just to go hide behind some tree-line and wait for you to think he left.  That... is amazing, and definitely the highlight.  Whoever coded the t-rex behavior deserves a big, warmed chocolate chip cookie with some milk.\n\nI know the developer(s) has put intention into the behavior of all the dinosaurs, making them different from each other, but the t-rex is the only one that feels.... alive, and gives me goosebumps.  My recommendation is that more of the other predators have, some sort of stalking/elusive behavior as well.  Maybe the raptors can intelligently try to surround you before raising alarm (pack behavior), and try to hide behind bushes.  Maybe some only move when they think you aren't looking (weeping angel mechanic?).  Or hungry, determined predators can slowly track your footsteps, over miles.  That's a scary, but exciting thought!  Just some ideas, not saying all of these need to be added, but the t-rex is 90% the reason I keep coming back to this game.\n\nGameplay & optimization are more important than graphics, and I think that is the right thing to prioritize in a game.  I am tired of cinematic, pretty games that are boring or stutter and crash. \n\n(EDIT:  I used to dunk on the graphics [calling them 2008] because I didn't see options to change them while in session, but you can tweak the graphics in the main menu.  With the graphic on the higher side, it doesn't feel too bad [2013 style?], but I still think you should use ReShade.  If you download and use ReShade, the graphics in this game can work a bit better.  I added some sharpening & crt filter, then some ambient light, pinch of bloom, and some other color contrast ones.) \n\nAnother thing that is amazing, and I was astonished by: you can CREATE you OWN ISLAND.  I don't think I personally have the time and patience to do this, but someone could!  \n\nThe devs are doing great so far, very impressed. ",
